;; TON PRIMES — Ratchet message schemas.
;; Opcodes: /shared/opcodes.ts `RATCHET`. Contract: contracts/primes_ratchet.tolk.
;;
;; Split out of bank.tlb (now ledger.tlb) by UPGRADE_PLAN_V5.md §4 B1 /
;; CONCEPT v5 §3.4, which puts `pending`, `flush()`, the DeDust leg and the burn
;; on their own contract so that "there is no admin withdraw path" is verifiable
;; by reading ONE contract's outbound set.
;;
;; That outbound set, in full:
;;   1. dedust_swap#ea06185d, destination = the venue address, changeable only
;;      by the venue timelock;
;;   2. burn#595f07bc (TEP-74), destination = whichever jetton wallet sent the
;;      transfer_notification, amount = exactly what it notified (§4.3's
;;      proceeds leg — bought PRIMES are burned, never held; `S` is untouched,
;;      §3.3);
;;   3. deposit#47d54391, destination = the Tonstakers pool (§4.3.2's staked
;;      reserve, D-151), amount = the message value, which the contract sizes
;;      itself from `reserve_target` — no message below lets a sender name it;
;;   4. burn#595f07bc again, destination = this contract's OWN tsTON wallet,
;;      carrying §4.3.2's withdrawal payload — the redeemed TON returns here and
;;      can return nowhere else, because the pool pays the burner;
;;   5. nothing else.
;; No withdraw, no sweep, no owner field, and no message any sender can send
;; that moves TON to an address of the sender's choosing. `flush` is
;; permissionless precisely because it cannot be aimed, and the three staking
;; triggers keep that property in a stronger form: they carry no size field at
;; all.
;;
;; Trust edges:
;;   - pool_inject: sender MUST equal the ledger's address (set once, by
;;     set_ledger, from the genesis script).
;;   - flush: permissionless, any sender.
;;   - update_venue: sender MUST equal the venue timelock's fixed address. This
;;     is the entire admin surface of this contract, and it can change the swap
;;     DESTINATION but never the amount, the recipient of the proceeds, the
;;     floor guard, or §4.3.2's staking addresses (D-194: deploy data). The
;;     timelock sends it only after 30 days AND a passed LP vote, because a
;;     re-pointed vault receives every flush tranche.
;;   - set_ledger: sender MUST equal the genesis script; one-shot.
;;   - transfer_notification: deliberately UNCHECKED sender — the only reply is
;;     a burn aimed back at that same sender for the notified amount, riding
;;     the notification's own attached gas, so a forged notification can only
;;     burn the forger's own tokens and moves no protocol TON. Since §4.3.2 this
;;     message arrives from TWO wallets and the handler branches on which stored
;;     address the sender matches — see the tsTON block below.
;;   - stake / unstake / harvest: permissionless, any sender, and unlike `flush`
;;     with no size field to lower (§4.3.2, §11.14).
;;   - set_tston_wallet: sender MUST equal the genesis script; one-shot.
;;   - ownership_assigned / distributed_asset: UNAUTHENTICATABLE by construction —
;;     the payout-bill collection's address is salted at deploy, so there is no
;;     address to check against. §4.3.2's block below is the argument for why a
;;     forged one is a donation rather than an attack; it is the load-bearing
;;     paragraph of that section.

;; ---- inbound: TEP-74 proceeds notification -> outbound burn (§4.3) ----

transfer_notification#7362d09c
    query_id:uint64
    amount:Coins
    sender:MsgAddress
    forward_payload:(Either Cell ^Cell)
  = InternalMsgBody;

burn#595f07bc
    query_id:uint64
    amount:Coins
    response_destination:MsgAddress   ;; the ratchet itself: excesses feed its gas buffer
    custom_payload:(Maybe ^Cell)      ;; none$0 on THIS leg; §4.3.2's tsTON burn fills it
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; One constructor, two destinations. Aimed at the PRIMES wallet it is §4.3's
;; proceeds burn; aimed at the tsTON wallet, with the payload present, it is
;; §4.3.2's withdrawal request. Nothing else distinguishes them on the wire.
;;
;; CLOSED by P2 of D-151, 2026-09-21. Both sides now carry the field:
;; primes_ratchet.tolk's `JettonBurn` writes `customPayload: cell?` (null on the
;; PRIMES leg, the two withdrawal bits on the tsTON one) and
;; primes_jetton_wallet.tolk's `Burn` parses and ignores it. Until then neither
;; side wrote or read it -- the same defect GAUNTLET V4 fixed on `Transfer` and
;; `transfer_notification` in that file (see its header at :62-74), missed on
;; `Burn` because nothing external burns PRIMES. The pair was self-consistent, so
;; the PRIMES burn worked and nothing showed it. §4.3.2 is what made it bite: the
;; tsTON wallet is somebody else's contract, its `load_dict()` underflows on the
;; missing Maybe bit, and the burn dies in the ACTION phase.

;; ---- inbound: the pool backing, from the ledger only ----
;; See pool_inject in ledger.tlb for the full field semantics; the layout is
;; identical and is repeated here only so this file stands alone.

pool_inject#b0000050
    query_id:uint64
    amount:Coins          ;; NOMINAL backing; `pending` is credited by THIS, not by msg value
    t_snapshot:Coins      ;; T after the sending mint — nanotons
    s_snapshot:Coins      ;; S after the sending mint — nano-PRIMES
  = InternalMsgBody;

;; ---- inbound: permissionless flush trigger (concept §4.3) ----

flush#b0000006
    query_id:uint64
    amount:Coins          ;; a CEILING the caller may lower, never raise. 0 = "size it yourself"
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; No value required beyond covering the triggering message's own forward fee;
;; swap gas is paid by the ratchet from its own accumulated hop-margin surplus,
;; which is exactly the `delta` its get_solvency reports.
;;
;; Guards, all pre-existing and unchanged: FLUSH_COOLDOWN since the last
;; attempt (error 207), and the auto size `min(pending, max(pending * FLUSH_PCT,
;; FLUSH_MIN))` (error 208).
;;
;; `amount` (2026-09-19) is VENUE-DEPTH SIZING. The auto size is computed from
;; `pending` and knows nothing about the pool; `min_out = amount * S / T` is
;; priced off the pool. Nothing connected the two, so a tranche bigger than the
;; venue could absorb bounced against a guard that was working as designed --
;; the swap's AVERAGE execution price ran above `p_f` while the SPOT price sat
;; below it. The contract cannot read DeDust's reserves (no synchronous
;; cross-contract call on TVM, and `get_reserves` is a get-method), so the
;; caller computes the fillable size with `shared/dedustQuote.ts`'s
;; `maxInAtFloor` -- a_max = T*R_p/(S*(1+G)) - R_t/(1-f) -- and sends it here.
;;
;; The handler takes `min(amount, autoSize)`: one-directional, so a caller may
;; only ask for LESS than the contract would have sent. That is what makes a
;; permissionless size safe -- `min_out` is LINEAR in `amount`, so the guard is
;; a per-unit price and no choice of `amount` buys a nanoton above `p_f`. The
;; cooldown is charged pro rata (`cooldown * amount / autoSize`) so a dust-sized
;; call cannot spend the day's throughput allowance and lock out a real flush.

;; ---- inbound: venue address update, from the venue timelock only ----

update_venue#b0000010
    query_id:uint64
    dedust_native_vault:MsgAddress
    dedust_pool:MsgAddress
    primes_jetton_vault:MsgAddress
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; D-194: the staking block below is NOT on this message. It is ratchet deploy
;; data (the `staking` ref of the stored venue) and `update_venue` copies it
;; forward unchanged: `stake()` sends `pending - reserve` to `tonstakers_pool` in
;; one message, so an address that could be re-pointed would be a withdraw path.
;;
;; Audit R2-M1 (2026-11-19): `update_venue` is NEVER refused for a flush in flight.
;; The stored venue instead keeps that trip's two reply addresses until the next
;; flush is sent, so the OLD vault's refund or fill still settles it:

flush_trip#_
    dedust_native_vault:MsgAddress   ;; pays the refusal (payout#474f86cf)
    primes_jetton_vault:MsgAddress   ;; pays the fill (the jetton-level sender)
  = FlushTrip;

;; The stored venue (ratchet data, not a message). `open_trip` is null except
;; between an `update_venue` that landed mid-flight and the next flush.
venue#_
    dedust_native_vault:MsgAddress
    dedust_pool:MsgAddress
    primes_jetton_vault:MsgAddress
    staking:^VenueStaking
    open_trip:(Maybe ^FlushTrip)
  = Venue;

;; §4.3.2's venue, as a REF CHAIN rather than four more inline fields. SEVEN
;; venue addresses is 1,869 bits and a cell holds 1,023, so the split is
;; arithmetic and not taste: three inline, three here, one more ref for the
;; treasury.

venue_staking#_
    tonstakers_pool:MsgAddress       ;; deposit#47d54391's destination
    dedust_tston_vault:MsgAddress    ;; leg 2's TEP-74 transfer destination (the JETTON vault)
    dedust_tston_pool:MsgAddress     ;; leg 2's SwapStep.pool_addr
    treasury:^VenueTreasury
  = VenueStaking;

venue_treasury#_
    treasury_addr:MsgAddress         ;; the LP-governed treasury; harvest()'s ONLY destination
    dedust_tston_native_vault:MsgAddress ;; leg 2's payout#474f86cf sender: the tsTON pool's
                                     ;; own factory's native vault, NOT the re-pointable
                                     ;; venue's (2026-11-08 audit)
  = VenueTreasury;
;; `dedust_tston_vault` is NOT in D-151's field list and is added by P2, because
;; a jetton-side DeDust swap needs both addresses and they are different
;; contracts: the tsTON goes to the VAULT for that jetton (as the TEP-74
;; transfer's `destination`) while the POOL is named inside the swap payload's
;; `SwapStep.pool_addr`. The native leg above hides this because `dedust_swap`
;; carries both too -- `Venue.dedustNativeVault` as the message destination and
;; `Venue.dedustPool` inside the body. Same shape, one asset over.
;;
;; Retargeted from the ledger to here by §3.4: the venue belongs with the leg
;; that uses it. Sender MUST equal the venue timelock's fixed, immutable address
;; — see engineering-spec §6 for why this is not itself an admin escape hatch,
;; and §11.1/§11.3 (still open) for immutable-vs-timelock.

;; ---- inbound: one-shot ledger bootstrap, from the genesis script only ----

set_ledger#b0000051
    query_id:uint64
    ledger_addr:MsgAddress
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; Circular address dependency, same as the ledger's set_peers/set_sinks. Until
;; it lands, pool_inject throws 101; a second set_ledger throws 104.

;; ---- inbound: one-shot burn-counter bootstrap, from the genesis script only ----

set_jetton_wallet#b0000052
    query_id:uint64
    jetton_wallet_addr:MsgAddress
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; §3.3 / §9.1 / §11.21. This contract's own PRIMES wallet is addressed by its
;; owner, so it cannot live in this contract's address-determining data — the
;; same circular dependency set_ledger has, and the same one-shot resolution the
;; bounty vault uses for its own wallet (bounty_vault.tlb, D21).
;;
;; What it is FOR: the burn itself deliberately has no sender check (see
;; transfer_notification below), because burning is the only thing this contract
;; ever does with jettons and a forged notification can therefore do no harm. A
;; *counter* is different — an unchecked one could be inflated by anyone, and it
;; is published on the dashboard as measured. So the burn stays unchecked and the
;; TALLY is checked: only a notification from this address increments it. Until
;; this lands, `getBurnStats` returns counting = 0 and the total is a floor, not
;; a measurement; a second set_jetton_wallet throws 104.

;; ---- outbound: the swap, to the DeDust native vault ----

;; GAUNTLET V2 (2026-09-09): this is DeDust v2's OWN published schema
;; (docs.dedust.io/reference/tlb-schemes, fetched 2026-09-09), not this repo's
;; invention. The pre-V2 version of this file documented `dedust_swap#ea06185d
;; query_id:uint64 min_out:Coins` and nothing else — no `pool_addr`, no
;; `swap_params` — because the code it described never sent them either,
;; carried over from primes_bank.tolk D6 as "the real wire format could not be
;; verified." It is checkably wrong, not merely unverified: a vault cannot
;; resolve which pool to swap through with no `pool_addr` field at all.
swap_kind_given_in$0 = SwapKind;         ;; the only kind DeDust implements
swap_kind_given_out$1 = SwapKind;        ;; not live on DeDust's side; never sent here

swap_step_params#_
    kind:SwapKind
    limit:Coins                          ;; nano-PRIMES — §4.3.1's FLOOR GUARD
    next:(Maybe ^SwapStep)               ;; always none$0 here — single-hop only
  = SwapStepParams;

swap_step#_
    pool_addr:MsgAddressInt              ;; `Venue.dedustPool` — stored, previously unread by flush()
    params:SwapStepParams
  = SwapStep;

swap_params#_
    deadline:uint32                      ;; blockchain.now() + DEDUST_SWAP_DEADLINE_WINDOW (600s)
    recipient_addr:MsgAddressInt         ;; this contract's own address — the fill lands back here
    referral_addr:MsgAddress             ;; addr_none — no referral fee on this leg
    fulfill_payload:(Maybe ^Cell)        ;; always none$0
    reject_payload:(Maybe ^Cell)         ;; always none$0
  = SwapParams;

dedust_swap#ea06185d
    query_id:uint64
    amount:Coins                         ;; the TON side of the swap; equals the message value
    step:SwapStep                        ;; inline, not a ref — matches DeDust's `_:SwapStep`
    swap_params:^SwapParams
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; B3, and the whole of §4.3.1's claim, is `limit` (nested inside `SwapStepParams`
;; above, not a top-level field). Pre-v5 it was the literal 1 ("accept any fill at
;; any price"). It is now
;;
;;     limit = amount * S / T * (1 + FLOOR_GUARD)
;;
;; UNITS: `amount` is nanotons, `T` is nanotons, `S` is nano-PRIMES, so `T / S`
;; is the floor price p_f in nanotons per nano-PRIMES and `amount * S / T` is
;; nano-PRIMES — which is what this field is denominated in. The two nano-scales
;; cancel; there is no 1e9 factor to insert, and inserting one misprices by a
;; billion. The multiply is done BEFORE the divide and both operands are
;; asserted below 2^120 (product < 2^240, clear of TVM's 2^256), because the
;; naive `(amount / T) * S` truncates to zero for every realistic flush and would
;; silently reproduce the bug this field exists to fix.
;;
;; FLOOR_GUARD is a Phase A output and is currently 0 — the NEUTRAL element of
;; the formula, i.e. §3 step 7's stated measurement baseline `amount * S / T`,
;; not a guess.
;;
;; NOT ROUND-TRIP PROVEN against a live or schema-faithful counterparty — see
;; GAUNTLET.md V2's closing note and `WireFormatDeDustSwap.spec.ts`, which
;; parses the real outbound cell field-by-field against this schema instead.
;;
;; The message is sent with BounceMode.RichBounce. When the venue cannot fill at
;; or above min_out the TON comes back and `pending` is restored unchanged — but
;; by TWO different routes, and this file used to name only one of them. The
;; guard adds no parallel path either way, which is why a would-be-above-floor
;; flush and a sandwiched one are indistinguishable at contract level, and why
;; get_flush_status below is not optional.

;; ---- inbound: the refund, from the DeDust native vault ----
;;
;; A REFUSED SWAP DOES NOT BOUNCE. DeDust checks `limit` in the POOL, one hop
;; past the vault; the pool commits its refund and then throws (exit 304,
;; SLIPPAGE_EXCEEDED, aborted FALSE), so the vault's own transaction succeeds —
;; and a TON bounce travels exactly one hop, leaving the `dedust_swap` above
;; nothing to bounce from. Verified against mainnet 2026-09-16 (trace
;; 0vgUx3EUXuoH3l9kfjvHfxkO8khDq5qw1iTE58hdV5M=, and 6,000 consecutive
;; native-vault transactions with zero bounced inbound messages). GAUNTLET.md
;; R18 carries the evidence and what assuming otherwise cost.
;;
;; `payload` is the sender's own `reject_payload` handed back verbatim: the pool
;; copies it into pay_out_from_pool#ad4eb6f5 and the vault copies it into this
;; message. The ratchet sends `reject_payload = none`, so it is always none here
;; and is consumed as an opaque remainder rather than parsed.
;;
;; The bounce handler is KEPT alongside this one and is not redundant: it covers
;; the VAULT refusing outright (too little gas, unrecognised op), which fails one
;; hop closer and therefore really does bounce.
dedust_payout#474f86cf
    query_id:uint64                      ;; echoed from dedust_swap, unchanged
    payload:(Maybe ^Cell)                ;; our reject_payload, verbatim
  = InternalMsgBody;

;; ======== §4.3.2: the staked reserve, and the Tonstakers wire (D-151) ========
;;
;; PROVENANCE: every upstream constructor below is read from
;; github.com/ton-blockchain/liquid-staking-contract @ `main`, fetched
;; 2026-09-20, with the file and line cited on each one. The protocol's GitBook
;; documents behaviour but publishes NO opcodes, so the source tree is the only
;; authority there is — an opcode here has been checked against the code and
;; against nothing else, because nothing else states it.
;;
;; It lives in this file, not in a `tonstakers.tlb`, for the same reason the
;; DeDust block above does: an external layout belongs in the schema of the
;; contract that talks to it.
;;
;; CAVEAT, and it is the half that dates fastest. The pool's ECONOMIC behaviour —
;; the deposit fee, round length, whether deposits are open at all — is
;; governor-settable storage and can change under us with no redeploy and no
;; notice. The WIRE values below (opcodes, field order, bit order) are baked into
;; the deployed code and cannot. So: read a FIGURE quoted in these comments (the
;; 1 TON DEPOSIT_FEE) as storage as it stood on the fetch date; read an OPCODE as
;; fixed for the life of that deployment.

;; ---- inbound: the three permissionless staking triggers (§4.3.2) ----

stake#b0000053
    query_id:uint64
  = InternalMsgBody;

unstake#b0000054
    query_id:uint64
  = InternalMsgBody;

harvest#b0000055
    query_id:uint64
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; NONE OF THE THREE CARRIES AN AMOUNT, and that absence is the point: §11.14's
;; constraint made structural rather than asserted. Each leg's size is computed
;; by the contract from `reserve_target` against the current balances, so a
;; caller can TRIGGER the rule and cannot AIM it — there is no destination field
;; and no size field to supply. `flush` above is permissionless because its one
;; number may only be lowered; these are permissionless because they have no
;; number at all.
;;
;; UNVERIFIED in this file: nothing below carries the Tonstakers POOL address —
;; no message here proposes or updates it. This schema records the wire only.

;; ---- inbound: the stranded-exit recovery arm (D-155) ----

recover_exit#b0000057
    query_id:uint64
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; NO AMOUNT EITHER, and for a stronger reason than the three above: this one
;; cannot move value at all. Its whole effect is to run the contract's internal
;; `abort_exit` -- `exit_cost_basis` goes from `exiting` back to `staked_cost`
;; (both terms of `T`, so `T` is unchanged), the tsTON claim returns to
;; `tston_held`, and `pending` is not written. There is nothing to aim and
;; nothing to gain, which is what lets it be permissionless.
;; A STRANDED STAKE (a deposit whose mint notification has not landed by
;; now - stake_started_at >= recover_after; get_staking_gates' fourth value)
;; is written off instead: its cost NOT YET MATCHED by arrived tsTON
;; (in_flight_stake - stake_credited, get_staking_gates' sixth value) leaves
;; `staked_cost` as `realized_loss` and accumulates in `written_off`, so
;; stake/unstake (230) are not locked for ever. Checked before the exit. The
;; next authenticated fill restores `written_off` from the value of the tsTON
;; already held at the rate it teaches. A recovered leg 1/2 exit's late reply
;; is recognised and not counted again.
;;
;; WHY IT EXISTS. Every transition of the exit machine is driven by an INBOUND
;; REPLY, and until D-155 `exit_leg` returned to idle only when one arrived. A
;; reply that is lost, malformed, under-gassed in a way that does not bounce, or
;; sent by an address a sender gate rejects, left the machine outstanding
;; forever -- and `flush#b0000006` refuses while an exit is open (230), so a lost
;; reply bricked the ratchet's own core loop, not merely the staking arms. Proved
;; live on testnet 2026-09-21 with a leg 2 payout from the wrong vault address.
;;
;; THE WINDOW IS 72 HOURS and its floor is set by leg 3: the pessimistic bill
;; pays at ROUND END, ~36 h (`staked_reserve.leg3_latency_days` = 1.5), so
;; anything at or under that would abort a leg that is merely waiting its turn.
;; 2x the round means the arm can only fire on a reply that has stopped coming.
;; Divided by the same D-139 `clock_divisor` as the flush cooldown and the
;; harvest interval; get_exit_leg publishes the DIVIDED figure, so a reader
;; checks the window this deploy actually enforces. Refuses 232
;; (ratchet_exit_not_stranded) before it.

;; ---- inbound: one-shot tsTON-wallet bootstrap, from the genesis script only ----

set_tston_wallet#b0000056
    query_id:uint64
    tston_wallet:MsgAddress
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; Exactly the shape of set_jetton_wallet#b0000052 above, for exactly the same
;; reason: this contract's tsTON wallet is addressed by its OWNER, so it cannot
;; live in this contract's own address-determining data. One-shot, refused on the
;; same flag a second time, and inert afterwards — a used-up bootstrap
;; credential, not an admin key. See that constructor's note for the full
;; circular-dependency argument; it is not repeated here.

;; ---- outbound: the deposit, to the Tonstakers pool ----
;; contracts/interaction.tlb:52; op at contracts/op-codes.func:41.

deposit#47d54391
    query_id:uint64
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; The staked amount is the MESSAGE VALUE — the body has no amount field. The
;; pool subtracts a flat DEPOSIT_FEE = 1 TON from the value and credits the
;; remainder (contracts/pool.func:79, :288-290), then
;; `throw_unless(error::deposit_amount_too_low, deposit_amount > 0)`. A deposit
;; of 1 TON or less therefore THROWS rather than doing nothing, so `stake` must
;; size its tranche clear of the fee, and the fee is a real cost of the leg, not
;; a rounding artefact: it is spent TON that never reaches `tston_held`.

;; ---- outbound: the withdrawal, as a TEP-74 burn of our own tsTON ----
;;
;; We do NOT send the pool's `withdraw` below. We send burn#595f07bc (declared at
;; the top of this file) to our own tsTON wallet, with the two withdrawal flags
;; riding in `custom_payload` as TWO BARE BITS in this order —
;; wrappers/JettonWallet.ts:17-19:

tston_withdraw_payload#_
    wait_till_round_end:Bool             ;; bit 0
    fill_or_kill:Bool                    ;; bit 1
  = TstonWithdrawPayload;

;; The jetton minter then inlines those two bits into the message it sends the
;; pool, which is why the order above is load-bearing and why the pool-side
;; layout is recorded here too — contracts/interaction.tlb:54:

withdraw#319b0cdc
    query_id:uint64
    jetton_amount:Coins
    from_address:MsgAddress
    response_address:MsgAddress
    wait_till_round_end:Bool             ;; our payload's bit 0, verbatim
    fill_or_kill:Bool                    ;; our payload's bit 1, verbatim
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; Reading the two together is the only way to see that swapping our two bits
;; swaps the pool's two flags: a transposed payload asks for the opposite
;; withdrawal (kill an unfillable one instead of waiting out the round, or wait
;; where we meant to be filled now) and every message on the way still parses.
;;
;; THE TRAP, and it fails silently. `custom_payload` is optional in TEP-74 and
;; mandatory here. The pool reads the two flags with `load_bool` inside its own
;; try/catch; an absent or short payload underflows, the catch fires, and the
;; pool RE-MINTS the jettons back to us. A burn with no payload is a ROUND TRIP,
;; not a withdrawal — no throw we can see, no bounce, the tsTON back in the
;; wallet and no TON coming. Any check that reads "the burn succeeded" as "the
;; withdrawal started" is wrong in exactly that case.

;; ---- inbound: the filled withdrawal, from the pool ----
;; contracts/interaction.tlb:55; op at contracts/op-codes.func:43.

withdrawal#0a77535c
    query_id:uint64
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; Non-bounceable, sent CARRY_ALL_BALANCE. THE BODY CARRIES NO AMOUNT: the filled
;; TON is the message value, and it arrives mixed with whatever is left of the
;; gas we attached to our own burn. So this message can never tell us what rate
;; we exited at — the received value is a payout plus change, and the two are not
;; separable at arrival. That is precisely why the tsTON rate is learned from a
;; DEPOSIT's transfer_notification, where TON sent and jettons minted are both
;; known exactly, and never from a withdrawal.

;; ---- inbound: the payout bill, when the withdrawal cannot be filled ----
;; contracts/payout_nft/nft-item.func:193-197; op at payout_nft/op-codes.func:3.

ownership_assigned#05138d91
    query_id:uint64
    prev_owner:MsgAddress                ;; addr_none — two zero bits
    forward_payload:(Either Cell ^Cell)  ;; empty
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; Arrives when the round could not fill our withdrawal and `fill_or_kill` was 0:
;; the pool mints a payout BILL NFT to us instead of paying. Non-bounceable,
;; CARRY_ALL_BALANCE | IGNORE_ERRORS.

;; ---- inbound: the bill paying out at round end ----
;; contracts/payout_nft/nft-collection.func:262-268; op at payout_nft/op-codes.func:15.

distributed_asset#db3b8abd
    query_id:uint64
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; Non-bounceable, CARRY_ALL_REMAINING_MESSAGE_VALUE, our TON share = the message
;; value. Again no amount field: the money IS the message.
;;
;; WHY NEITHER OF THE LAST TWO IS AUTHENTICATED, AND WHY THAT IS SAFE. The payout
;; bill collection's address is SALTED with `random()` at deploy
;; (contracts/address_calculations.func:67-68). It is therefore not derivable
;; from the pool's address, cannot be precomputed at genesis, and cannot be
;; whitelisted afterwards without a message to set it — which would be a new
;; admin surface on a contract whose whole claim is that it has none. The ratchet
;; consequently CANNOT check the sender of either message, and does not pretend
;; to. What it does instead is make a forgery arithmetically a donation:
;;   - ownership_assigned takes NO accounting action whatsoever. Nothing is
;;     credited, nothing is unlocked, nothing is counted. The outstanding exit is
;;     tracked by the `exiting` that our own `unstake` credited, not by the
;;     arrival of an NFT anyone can send us. A forged one changes no number.
;;   - distributed_asset credits `pending` and draws `exiting` down by the same
;;     value. BOTH ARE TERMS OF `T`, they move in the directions a real payout
;;     moves them, and the TON backing the credit is really in this contract's
;;     balance because it is the message value itself. A forged one is therefore
;;     a stranger paying into the pool on a bill's behalf: it mints no PRIMES,
;;     aims no TON, cannot be replayed for value the sender did not attach, and
;;     cannot push `pending` above the balance that backs it. The attack costs
;;     the attacker the exact amount it gives the ratchet.
;; This is the same argument as the unchecked transfer_notification at the top of
;; the file — the reply is harmless to the protocol and expensive to the forger,
;; so authentication would buy nothing worth an address dependency for.

;; ---- inbound: the SECOND jetton wallet (tsTON), same two ops as the first ----
;;
;; excesses#d53276db and transfer_notification#7362d09c now reach this contract
;; from TWO wallets — the PRIMES wallet of set_jetton_wallet#b0000052 and the
;; tsTON wallet of set_tston_wallet#b0000056. The layouts are unchanged (the
;; notification is declared at the top of this file); what changes is that the
;; notification handler MUST branch on which stored address the sender matches:
;;   - tsTON wallet, exact match -> §4.3.2's deposit fill. Credit `tston_held`,
;;     update the learned rate from (TON sent, jettons minted) ONLY when the
;;     TEP-74 `from` is addr_none (the pool's optimistic mint). Any other tsTON
;;     that is not an exit leg's answer — a NON-OPTIMISTIC fill, which arrives
;;     as a transfer from the salted payout collection, or a stranger's
;;     transfer — is valued at the last learned rate and credited against the
;;     open stake (`stake_credited`) and then any write-off (audit H2). NEVER burn it:
;;     burning tsTON WITHOUT the withdrawal payload is the round trip described
;;     above, and burning it WITH one is a withdrawal nobody asked for.
;;   - anything else -> the pre-existing unchecked burn-back, unchanged, which
;;     burns only the sender's own tokens; the counted tally still moves only for
;;     the PRIMES wallet.
;; A handler that forgets the branch burns the reserve as fast as it is staked,
;; so the branch is the whole of the safety here.

excesses#d53276db
    query_id:uint64
  = InternalMsgBody;
;; Accepted and ignored, from EITHER wallet, the way a bare top-up is. It is
;; named here because this contract writes its own address into
;; `response_destination` on both burn legs and must therefore have an arm for
;; the reply — the catch-all `else => assert (in.body.isEmpty()) throw 101` would
;; otherwise abort it and paint the trace red while the money looks right
;; (MISTAKES.md 2026-09-17).

;; ---- get-methods (§9.1's honesty constraint: every UI number maps to one) ----
;;
;; get_pending          -> Coins
;; get_swapped          -> Coins            ;; §5's `T = swapped + pending` identity
;; get_in_flight_flush  -> Coins
;; get_last_min_out     -> Coins            ;; the guard price the last attempt demanded
;; get_floor_cache      -> (Coins, Coins)   ;; the pushed (T, S) mirror, staleness visible
;; get_venue            -> (MsgAddress, MsgAddress, MsgAddress)
;; get_ledger           -> (MsgAddress, uint8)
;; get_last_flush_ts    -> uint64
;;
;; get_burn_stats -> (burned_primes:Coins, burn_events:uint32, counting:uint8)
;;   §3.3 / §11.21 — the burn half of the circulating-supply report, which must be
;;   shown as its own number next to the vault's payout total rather than netted
;;   into it. Counts what THIS contract ordered burned (the §4.3 flush leg), so it
;;   is not the same quantity as `S − total_supply`: that difference also carries
;;   the vault's unspent tokens and any bounced rebate mint, and the dashboard
;;   shows it separately and labelled as derived.
;;   `counting` is 0 until set_jetton_wallet lands. At 0 the total is a FLOOR, not
;;   a measurement — returned rather than inferred so that "nothing burned yet"
;;   and "not counting yet" cannot be confused, the same distinction
;;   get_flush_status draws between `never` and an undeployed method.
;;
;; get_burn_wallet -> (MsgAddress, uint8)
;;   The wallet whose notifications are counted, checkable from outside against
;;   the jetton master's get_wallet_address(ratchet).
;;
;; get_flush_status -> (last_attempt_time:uint64, outcome:uint8,
;;                      amount:Coins, cooldown_remaining:uint32)
;;   outcome: 0 = never, 1 = filled, 2 = bounced.
;;   §12 makes this NON-OPTIONAL: without it "a dead venue hides behind months
;;   of legitimate-looking non-execution", and "the venue is gone" is
;;   indistinguishable from "the market is above the floor".
;;   `filled` is set optimistically at send time and demoted to `bounced` by the
;;   bounce handler — TVM offers no fill callback on this leg, so "did not
;;   bounce" is the only positive fill signal that exists. A reader who wants
;;   certainty checks get_in_flight_flush: non-zero means the round trip is
;;   still open and `filled` is provisional.
;;
;; get_staking -> (staked_cost:Coins, tston_held:Coins, exiting:Coins,
;;                 last_rate_num:uint64, last_rate_den:uint64,
;;                 reserve_target:Coins, last_harvest_time:uint32,
;;                 realized_loss:Coins)
;;   (wrapper: `getStaking()`.) §4.3.2's surface, and the reason it is one call
;;   rather than eight: §9.1 requires every number the dashboard shows about the
;;   staked reserve to be recomputable from chain state, and the dashboard shows
;;   all of these. `last_rate_num`/`last_rate_den` are published as the pair they
;;   were learned as — the deposit that taught them — so a reader can check the
;;   ratio rather than a rounded quotient we chose. `realized_loss` is reported
;;   next to `staked_cost` and never netted into it, the same separation
;;   get_burn_stats draws between what was burned and what was derived.
;;
;; get_flush_reply_venue -> (MsgAddress, MsgAddress, int)
;;   (wrapper: `getFlushReplyVenue()`.) Audit R2-M1: the native vault and PRIMES
;;   jetton vault that settle the OPEN flush, and `pinned` = 1 while an
;;   `update_venue` landed mid-flight and those are the OLD venue's. At 0 the
;;   pair is get_venue's own.
;;
;; get_staking_venue -> (MsgAddress, MsgAddress, MsgAddress)
;;   (wrapper: `getStakingVenue()`.) `tonstakers_pool`, `dedust_tston_vault`,
;;   `dedust_tston_pool`. A SEPARATE call from get_venue on purpose: that 3-tuple
;;   is read by the backend, the agent SDK and four test suites, and widening a
;;   published shape breaks every one of them where a second method breaks none.
;;
;; get_harvest_dest -> MsgAddress
;;   The governed treasury. Published on its own line because it is the only
;;   address here that RECEIVES protocol value rather than trading with it, which
;;   is what a reader checking §8 is looking for.
;;
;; get_tston_wallet -> (MsgAddress, uint8)
;;   The tsTON wallet whose notifications are the staking path, and whether the
;;   genesis script has wired it. Same shape and reason as get_burn_wallet:
;;   checkable from outside against the Tonstakers master's own
;;   get_wallet_address(ratchet). At 0 the three arms refuse by name (231).
;;
;; get_exit_leg -> (uint8, Coins, uint32, uint32)
;;   The exit state machine's leg (0 idle, 1 fill-or-kill burn, 2 the guarded
;;   DeDust swap, 3 the round-end bill) and the nano-tsTON inside it. Non-zero
;;   means `flush`, `stake` and `unstake` all refuse (230) -- see get_solvency's
;;   note on why an open round trip is fail-closed here.
;;   D-155 appends `exit_started_at` (0 while idle) and `recover_after`, the
;;   recovery window THIS DEPLOY enforces after `clock_divisor`. Together they
;;   answer "is this stranded, and when may anyone fix it":
;;   recover_exit#b0000057 is legal once now - exit_started_at >= recover_after.
;;   Appended rather than inserted, so the existing two-field readers are
;;   untouched.
;;
;; get_solvency -> (balance:Coins, liability:Coins, delta:int)
;;   §3.4's load-bearing property in ONE call. Liability here is `pending` and
;;   only `pending`: `in_flight_flush` has physically left this contract's
;;   balance and either fills or returns through the bounce handler, so counting
;;   it would make `delta` transiently negative for the whole round trip and turn
;;   Phase D's D7 reconciliation block into a daily false alarm.
